<h2>The facts and the trial</h2>
<p>A schoolgirl left home in December 2014 and did not return. The prosecution case was that the appellant, a neighbour known to the family, took her to Moradabad, Bengal and Akbarpur, assaulting her repeatedly; she was recovered in February 2015, about two months pregnant, and the pregnancy was terminated. The trial court convicted him under Sections 363 and 506 IPC and Section 6 POCSO, sentencing him in January 2025 to, among other terms, 30 years' simple imprisonment on the POCSO count. He had been in custody since 13 February 2015.</p>

<h2>What survived the appeal — and what did not</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Age proved.</strong> The school record showing a 2000 date of birth, consistent with the parents' testimony, established minority; the defence claim of a 1997 birth was not proved.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Testimony held reliable.</strong> Discrepancies about stations, recovery memos and locations were treated as investigative lapses, not contradictions going to the core of the account.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Presumptions applied.</strong> Sections 29 and 30 POCSO operate once foundational facts are laid — and they were, through the victim's evidence and the medical record.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Sentence reworked.</strong> 30 years' simple imprisonment became 15 years' rigorous imprisonment; fines and the concurrent IPC sentences were untouched.</div>
</div>

<h2>The retrospectivity point</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old">
    <h3>Law at the time of offence (2014-15)</h3>
    <p>Section 6 POCSO: rigorous imprisonment of not less than ten years, extendable to imprisonment for life, with fine.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new">
    <h3>Law after the 2019 amendment</h3>
    <p>Minimum raised to twenty years, extendable to imprisonment for the remainder of natural life, or death. Inapplicable to earlier offences by virtue of Article 20(1).</p>
  </div>
</div>
<p>The court anchored this in the Supreme Court's reasoning in <em>Satauram Mandavi</em>: an enhanced minimum enacted after the offence cannot inform the sentence. Sentencing courts dealing with historic POCSO offences must therefore locate the statute as it stood on the date of the offence — a point that is easy to miss when a trial concludes a decade later under a much-amended Act.</p>

<h2>Proportionality in long-delayed trials</h2>
<p>Equally significant is the court's insistence that a sentence far above the minimum needs reasons. The trial court's 30-year term was unexplained; the High Court weighed the aggravating circumstances — repeated assaults on a minor and their consequences — against the appellant's age at the time (about 25), the absence of similar antecedents and nearly eleven years already spent in custody, and fixed 15 years' rigorous imprisonment as proportionate within the statutory range. For appellate practice, the judgment illustrates that sentence appeals are not an afterthought to conviction appeals: the character of imprisonment, the applicable statutory version and the articulation of reasons are each independent grounds of challenge.</p>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> In any POCSO appeal arising from pre-2019 offences, verify three things against the date of occurrence — the applicable minimum, the character of imprisonment the section prescribes, and whether the trial court recorded reasons for exceeding the minimum. Set-off under the usual provisions for custody already undergone should be expressly sought, as it was granted here.</p>
</div>
